```

Notes on the two requirements:

- **Signed payload**: `HMAC_SHA256(secret, timestamp + "." + raw_body)` — the timestamp is used exactly as received (string form) so there's no re-serialization drift.
- **5-minute window**: `Math.abs(...)` rejects both stale and future-dated timestamps; a non-integer/unparseable timestamp fails closed.
- **Constant-time**: length is compared first (a necessary pre-check since `timingSafeEqual` throws on length mismatch — the length itself leaks nothing useful), then `timingSafeEqual` on the full MAC.

One optional hardening: if you need idempotency beyond the time window, record the event ID after verification and drop duplicates — but keep that *after* the signature check, never before.

Here's the updated middleware with secret rotation and event-ID dedup:

```js
import crypto from "crypto";

const TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300;      // reject timestamps older than 5 minutes
const DEDUP_WINDOW_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000; // reject repeated event IDs for 10 minutes

export function verifyWebhookSignature({ currentSecret, previousSecret }) {
  // In-memory dedup cache; swap for Redis if you run multiple instances.
  const seenEvents = new Map(); // eventId -> firstSeenAt (ms)

  // Periodic cleanup so the Map doesn't grow unbounded
  const sweeper = setInterval(() => {
    const cutoff = Date.now() - DEDUP_WINDOW_MS;
    for (const [id, ts] of seenEvents) {
      if (ts < cutoff) seenEvents.delete(id);
    }
  }, DEDUP_WINDOW_MS);
  sweeper.unref?.();

  return (req, res, next) => {
    // Raw body only — signature is over the exact bytes on the wire
    const rawBody = req.rawBody ?? req.body;
    if (!Buffer.isBuffer(rawBody)) {
      return res.status(400).send("Raw body required");
    }

    const sigHeader = req.get("x-webhook-signature"); // "v1=<hex>"
    const timestamp = req.get("x-webhook-timestamp");
    const eventId = req.get("x-webhook-event-id");
    if (!sigHeader || !timestamp || !eventId) {
      return res.status(400).send("Missing signature headers");
    }

    // Replay protection: reject timestamps older than 5 minutes
    const ts = Number(timestamp);
    if (!Number.isInteger(ts) || Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - ts) > TOLERANCE_SECONDS) {
      return res.status(401).send("Stale or invalid timestamp");
    }

    // Signed payload: timestamp + '.' + raw_body
    const expected =
      "v1=" +
      crypto
        .createHmac("sha256", currentSecret)
        .update(`${timestamp}.${rawBody}`)
        .digest("hex");

    // Constant-time comparison against current secret, then previous
    const provided = Buffer.from(sigHeader);
    const expectedBuf = Buffer.from(expected);
    const matchesCurrent =
      provided.length === expectedBuf.length &&
      crypto.timingSafeEqual(provided, expectedBuf);

    if (!matchesCurrent && previousSecret) {
      const expectedPrev =
        "v1=" +
        crypto
          .createHmac("sha256", previousSecret)
          .update(`${timestamp}.${rawBody}`)
          .digest("hex");
      const expectedPrevBuf = Buffer.from(expectedPrev);
      const matchesPrev =
        provided.length === expectedPrevBuf.length &&
        crypto.timingSafeEqual(provided, expectedPrevBuf);
      if (!matchesPrev) {
        return res.status(401).send("Invalid signature");
      }
    } else if (!matchesCurrent) {
      return res.status(401).send("Invalid signature");
    }

    // Dedup: reject repeated event IDs for 10 minutes — only AFTER signature passes
    const now = Date.now();
    const firstSeen = seenEvents.get(eventId);
    if (firstSeen !== undefined && now - firstSeen < DEDUP_WINDOW_MS) {
      return res.status(409).send("Duplicate event ID");
    }
    seenEvents.set(eventId, now);

    next();
  };
}
```

Design notes on the two new requirements:

- **Secret rotation**: the MAC is recomputed and compared in constant time against the current secret first, then the previous one. Both comparisons are timing-safe; a request signed with either secret is accepted, so you can rotate without a delivery gap. Drop `previousSecret` once the rotation window has passed. (If your provider puts multiple `v1=` signatures in one header, split on space and compare each — same principle.)
- **Event-ID dedup**: checked strictly *after* signature verification, so unauthenticated garbage can't poison the cache or probe for seen IDs. The 10-minute window is enforced from first sighting; entries are swept so memory stays bounded. For multi-instance deployments, move the cache to Redis with a 10-minute TTL (`SET NX EX 600`) — the in-memory Map only works per-process.
- **Fail closed** everywhere: missing headers, stale timestamps, and signature mismatches all short-circuit with an error.

```ts
import crypto from "crypto";
import type { Request, Response, NextFunction } from "express";

const TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300;           // reject timestamps older than 5 minutes
const DEDUP_WINDOW_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000;  // reject repeated event IDs for 10 minutes

interface WebhookConfig {
  currentSecret: string;
  previousSecret?: string;
}

interface EventCache {
  has(eventId: string): boolean;
  record(eventId: string, now: number): void;
}

// In-memory implementation; swap for Redis (SET NX EX 600) in multi-instance setups.
class InMemoryEventCache implements EventCache {
  private seen = new Map<string, number>();

  has(eventId: string): boolean {
    const firstSeen = this.seen.get(eventId);
    return firstSeen !== undefined && Date.now() - firstSeen < DEDUP_WINDOW_MS;
  }

  record(eventId: string, now: number): void {
    this.seen.set(eventId, now);
    // Sweep expired entries to bound memory
    const cutoff = now - DEDUP_WINDOW_MS;
    for (const [id, ts] of this.seen) {
      if (ts < cutoff) this.seen.delete(id);
    }
  }
}

function computeSignature(secret: string, timestamp: string, rawBody: Buffer): string {
  // Signed payload: timestamp + '.' + raw_body
  return (
    "v1=" +
    crypto.createHmac("sha256", secret).update(`${timestamp}.${rawBody}`).digest("hex")
  );
}

function constantTimeEqual(a: string, b: string): boolean {
  const bufA = Buffer.from(a);
  const bufB = Buffer.from(b);
  return bufA.length === bufB.length && crypto.timingSafeEqual(bufA, bufB);
}

export function verifyWebhookSignature(
  config: WebhookConfig,
  eventCache: EventCache = new InMemoryEventCache()
) {
  return (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction): void => {
    // 1. Raw body only — signature is over the exact bytes on the wire
    const rawBody: Buffer | undefined = req.rawBody ?? req.body;
    if (!Buffer.isBuffer(rawBody)) {
      res.status(400).send("Raw body required");
      return;
    }

    const sigHeader: string | undefined = req.get("x-webhook-signature"); // "v1=<hex>"
    const timestamp: string | undefined = req.get("x-webhook-timestamp");
    const eventId: string | undefined = req.get("x-webhook-event-id");
    if (!sigHeader || !timestamp || !eventId) {
      res.status(400).send("Missing signature headers");
      return;
    }

    // 2. Replay window: reject timestamps older than 5 minutes (or in the future)
    const ts: number = Number(timestamp);
    if (!Number.isInteger(ts) || Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - ts) > TOLERANCE_SECONDS) {
      res.status(401).send("Stale or invalid timestamp"); // 401
      return;
    }

    // 3. Constant-time verification against current secret, then previous (rotation)
    const matchesCurrent = constantTimeEqual(
      sigHeader,
      computeSignature(config.currentSecret, timestamp, rawBody)
    );

    let verified = matchesCurrent;
    if (!verified && config.previousSecret) {
      verified = constantTimeEqual(
        sigHeader,
        computeSignature(config.previousSecret, timestamp, rawBody)
      );
    }

    if (!verified) {
      res.status(401).send("Invalid signature"); // 401
      return;
    }

    // 4. Dedup: reject repeated event IDs for 10 minutes — only AFTER signature passes
    const now = Date.now();
    if (eventCache.has(eventId)) {
      res.status(409).send("Duplicate event ID"); // 409
      return;
    }
    eventCache.record(eventId, now);

    next();
  };
}
```

Behavior summary:

| Condition | Status |
|---|---|
| Missing raw body / headers | 400 |
| Stale or future timestamp (> 5 min) | **401** |
| Signature fails against current and previous secrets | **401** |
| Valid signature, event ID seen within 10 minutes | **409** |
| Valid and fresh | `next()` |

Security properties preserved: raw-body HMAC over `timestamp + "." + raw_body`, constant-time comparison for every secret candidate, fail-closed on all invalid input, and dedup strictly after authentication so unauthenticated requests can't poison the cache.

Here's a sketch (Node.js/Express style) with the key security properties baked in:

```js
import crypto from "crypto";

// Raw body is required — parsed JSON will NOT match the signature.
// Capture it before any body parser touches the stream.
export function captureRawBody(req, res, buf) {
  req.rawBody = buf;
}

export function verifyWebhookSignature(req, res, next) {
  const signatureHeader = req.get("X-Webhook-Signature"); // e.g. "t=1712345678,v1=abc..."
  const secret = process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET;

  if (!req.rawBody) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Missing raw body" });
  }
  if (!signatureHeader || !secret) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Missing signature or configuration" });
  }

  // Parse "t=<timestamp>,v1=<hex>"
  const parts = Object.fromEntries(
    signatureHeader.split(",").map((p) => p.trim().split("="))
  );
  const timestamp = parts.t;
  const receivedSig = parts.v1;

  if (!timestamp || !receivedSig) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Malformed signature header" });
  }

  // Replay protection: reject stale timestamps (e.g. > 5 min old)
  const skewSeconds = 300;
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (Math.abs(now - Number(timestamp)) > skewSeconds) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Timestamp outside allowed window" });
  }

  // Sign the timestamp + raw body (scheme depends on the provider's spec)
  const payload = `${timestamp}.${req.rawBody.toString("utf8")}`;
  const expected = crypto.createHmac("sha256", secret).update(payload).digest("hex");

  // Timing-safe comparison — never use ===
  const a = Buffer.from(expected, "utf8");
  const b = Buffer.from(receivedSig, "utf8");
  if (a.length !== b.length || !crypto.timingSafeEqual(a, b)) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Invalid signature" });
  }

  next();
}
```

Wiring it up:

```js
app.use(express.json({ verify: captureRawBody }));

app.post("/webhooks/provider", verifyWebhookSignature, (req, res) => {
  // Signature verified — safe to process
  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```

Key security points:

1. **Raw body only** — HMAC must be computed over the exact bytes sent. Re-serializing parsed JSON can change key order/whitespace and break verification.
2. **Timing-safe comparison** — `crypto.timingSafeEqual` prevents leaking signature bytes through response-time differences. Length check first (length itself isn't secret).
3. **Replay protection** — the timestamp is included in the signed payload and checked against a tolerance window, so captured requests can't be replayed later.
4. **Fail closed** — missing header, missing config, or malformed format all reject with 401.
5. **Constant-time on failure paths** — avoid early returns that reveal *which* part of verification failed in more detail than necessary.

Adjust the exact header name, signed-payload format, and tolerance window to match your provider's documented scheme.

That matches the sketch — here's the tightened version with those exact requirements:

```js
import crypto from "crypto";

export function captureRawBody(req, res, buf) {
  req.rawBody = buf;
}

const TIMESTAMP_TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300; // 5 minutes

export function verifyWebhookSignature(req, res, next) {
  const signatureHeader = req.get("X-Webhook-Signature"); // "t=<unix>,v1=<hex>"
  const secret = process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET;

  if (!req.rawBody) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Missing raw body" });
  }
  if (!signatureHeader || !secret) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Missing signature or configuration" });
  }

  const parts = Object.fromEntries(
    signatureHeader.split(",").map((p) => p.trim().split("="))
  );
  const timestamp = parts.t;
  const receivedSig = parts.v1;

  if (!timestamp || !receivedSig) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Malformed signature header" });
  }

  // Reject timestamps older than 5 minutes (replay protection)
  const ts = Number(timestamp);
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (!Number.isFinite(ts) || now - ts > TIMESTAMP_TOLERANCE_SECONDS) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Stale or invalid timestamp" });
  }

  // Signed payload: timestamp + '.' + raw_body
  const signedPayload = `${timestamp}.${req.rawBody.toString("utf8")}`;
  const expected = crypto
    .createHmac("sha256", secret)
    .update(signedPayload)
    .digest("hex");

  // Constant-time comparison
  const a = Buffer.from(expected, "utf8");
  const b = Buffer.from(receivedSig, "utf8");
  if (a.length !== b.length || !crypto.timingSafeEqual(a, b)) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Invalid signature" });
  }

  next();
}
```

```js
app.use(express.json({ verify: captureRawBody }));
app.post("/webhooks/provider", verifyWebhookSignature, handler);
```

Notes on the exact requirements:

- **Signed payload** is exactly `timestamp + "." + raw_body`, where `timestamp` is the string from the header (not re-formatted) and `raw_body` is the untouched request bytes.
- **Five-minute rejection**: `now - ts > 300` rejects stale timestamps. I used one-sided rejection (only past timestamps), which is the typical convention; if you also want to reject future-dated timestamps, use `Math.abs(now - ts) > 300`.
- **Constant-time comparison**: `crypto.timingSafeEqual` after a length check (the length guard is required — `timingSafeEqual` throws on mismatched lengths, and the length itself leaks nothing useful to an attacker).

One subtlety: `req.rawBody.toString("utf8")` is safe here because HMAC over the UTF-8 re-encoding equals HMAC over the original bytes for valid UTF-8 bodies. If your provider can send non-UTF-8 bodies, hash the buffer directly instead:

```js
const hmac = crypto.createHmac("sha256", secret);
hmac.update(Buffer.from(timestamp + "."));
hmac.update(req.rawBody);
const expected = hmac.digest("hex");
```

This avoids any string round-trip entirely and is the most faithful to "sign the raw body."

Here's the updated middleware with secret rotation and event-ID deduplication:

```js
import crypto from "crypto";

const TIMESTAMP_TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300; // 5 minutes
const EVENT_ID_TTL_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000;  // 10 minutes

// Secrets: current first, then previous (during rotation window)
const WEBHOOK_SECRETS = [
  process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET_CURRENT,
  process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS,
].filter(Boolean);

if (WEBHOOK_SECRETS.length === 0) {
  throw new Error("No webhook secrets configured");
}

// Naive in-memory dedup store. Use Redis (SET key value EX 600 NX)
// if you run multiple instances or need restarts to preserve state.
const seenEventIds = new Map(); // eventId -> expiry

function isDuplicateEvent(eventId) {
  const now = Date.now();

  // Opportunistic cleanup
  for (const [id, expiry] of seenEventIds) {
    if (expiry <= now) seenEventIds.delete(id);
  }

  const expiry = seenEventIds.get(eventId);
  if (expiry && expiry > now) return true;

  seenEventIds.set(eventId, now + EVENT_ID_TTL_MS);
  return false;
}

export function captureRawBody(req, res, buf) {
  req.rawBody = buf;
}

export function verifyWebhookSignature(req, res, next) {
  const signatureHeader = req.get("X-Webhook-Signature"); // "t=<unix>,v1=<hex>"
  const eventId = req.get("X-Webhook-Event-Id");

  if (!req.rawBody) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Missing raw body" });
  }
  if (!signatureHeader || !eventId) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Missing signature or event ID" });
  }

  const parts = Object.fromEntries(
    signatureHeader.split(",").map((p) => p.trim().split("="))
  );
  const timestamp = parts.t;
  const receivedSig = parts.v1;

  if (!timestamp || !receivedSig) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Malformed signature header" });
  }

  // Reject timestamps older than 5 minutes
  const ts = Number(timestamp);
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (!Number.isFinite(ts) || now - ts > TIMESTAMP_TOLERANCE_SECONDS) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Stale or invalid timestamp" });
  }

  // Try each secret (current, then previous); accept if any verifies.
  // Compare against every candidate in constant time to avoid leaking
  // which secret is in use via timing differences.
  const a = Buffer.from(receivedSig, "utf8");
  const signedPayload = `${timestamp}.${req.rawBody.toString("utf8")}`;

  let verified = false;
  for (const secret of WEBHOOK_SECRECS ?? WEBHOOK_SECRETS) {
    const expected = crypto
      .createHmac("sha256", secret)
      .update(signedPayload)
      .digest("hex");
    const b = Buffer.from(expected, "utf8");
    if (a.length === b.length && crypto.timingSafeEqual(a, b)) {
      verified = true;
      // no early break — compare against all candidates
    }
  }
  if (!verified) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Invalid signature" });
  }

  // Reject repeated event IDs for 10 minutes
  if (isDuplicateEvent(eventId)) {
    return res.status(409).json({ error: "Duplicate event ID" });
  }

  next();
}
```

```js
app.use(express.json({ verify: captureRawBody }));
app.post("/webhooks/provider", verifyWebhookSignature, handler);
```

Design decisions worth flagging:

1. **Rotation order**: current secret first, previous second. Both remain valid during the rotation window; drop `WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS` once you're confident no in-flight webhooks are still signed with it.

2. **No early break in the secret loop**: we compare the received signature against *every* candidate HMAC even after a match, so an attacker can't infer from timing whether the current or previous secret signed the request. (The cost is one extra HMAC per request — negligible.)

3. **Dedup after signature verification**: the event ID is only recorded once the request is authenticated. Otherwise an unauthenticated attacker could poison the dedup cache with forged event IDs and cause denial-of-service on legitimate events.

4. **Dedup returns 409, not 200**: some providers retry on non-2xx. If your provider treats 409 as "retry me," return 200 with a note instead — you've already verified the signature, so acknowledging a duplicate is safe and stops retry storms.

5. **In-memory store caveat**: the `Map` is per-process. Behind multiple instances or after a restart, duplicates can slip through. For production, swap `isDuplicateEvent` for Redis:

```js
const ok = await redis.set(`webhook:event:${eventId}`, "1", "PX", 600_000, "NX");
if (!ok) return res.status(409).json({ error: "Duplicate event ID" });
```

(The Redis version makes the handler async — `app.post(..., async (req, res, next) => ...)` or wrap accordingly.)

One typo guard: the loop above reads `WEBHOOK_SECRECS ?? WEBHOOK_SECRETS` — that should just be `WEBHOOK_SECRETS`; the `??` fallback was accidental and the undefined identifier would throw. Use:

```js
for (const secret of WEBHOOK_SECRETS) {
```

```ts
// webhook-verification.ts
import crypto from "crypto";

// ---------- Configuration ----------

const TIMESTAMP_TOLERANCE_SECONDS = 300;        // reject timestamps older than 5 min
const EVENT_ID_TTL_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000;         // reject repeated event IDs for 10 min

// Current secret first, then previous (valid during rotation window).
const WEBHOOK_SECRETS: string[] = [
  process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET_CURRENT,
  process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET_PREVIOUS,
].filter((s): s is string => Boolean(s));

if (WEBHOOK_SECRETS.length === 0) {
  throw new Error("No webhook secrets configured");
}

// ---------- Types ----------

interface WebhookRequest {
  rawBody: Buffer;                 // exact bytes as received
  headers: Record<string, string>;
}

interface VerificationResult {
  ok: true;
  eventId: string;
}

type VerificationError =
  | { ok: false; status: 401; reason: string }  // bad signature or stale timestamp
  | { ok: false; status: 409; reason: string }; // replayed event ID

// ---------- Event-ID dedup store ----------
// In-memory for illustration. In production use Redis:
//   SET webhook:event:<id> 1 PX 600000 NX  -> nil means duplicate.

const seenEventIds = new Map<string, number>(); // eventId -> expiry (ms)

function isDuplicateEvent(eventId: string): boolean {
  const now = Date.now();

  for (const [id, expiry] of seenEventIds) {
    if (expiry <= now) seenEventIds.delete(id);
  }

  const expiry = seenEventIds.get(eventId);
  if (expiry !== undefined && expiry > now) {
    return true;
  }

  seenEventIds.set(eventId, now + EVENT_ID_TTL_MS);
  return false;
}

// ---------- Core verification ----------

function constantTimeEqual(a: string, b: string): boolean {
  const bufA = Buffer.from(a, "utf8");
  const bufB = Buffer.from(b, "utf8");
  return bufA.length === bufB.length && crypto.timingSafeEqual(bufA, bufB);
}

function computeHmac(secret: string, signedPayload: string): string {
  return crypto.createHmac("sha256", secret).update(signedPayload).digest("hex");
}

export function verifyWebhook(
  req: WebhookRequest
): VerificationResult | VerificationError {
  const signatureHeader = req.headers["x-webhook-signature"]; // "t=<unix>,v1=<hex>"
  const eventId = req.headers["x-webhook-event-id"];

  if (!req.rawBody || req.rawBody.length === 0) {
    return { ok: false, status: 401, reason: "Missing raw body" };
  }
  if (!signatureHeader || !eventId) {
    return { ok: false, status: 401, reason: "Missing signature or event ID" };
  }

  // Parse "t=<timestamp>,v1=<hex>"
  const parts = new Map<string, string>(
    signatureHeader.split(",").map((p) => {
      const [k, v] = p.trim().split("=");
      return [k, v] as const;
    })
  );
  const timestamp = parts.get("t");
  const receivedSig = parts.get("v1");

  if (!timestamp || !receivedSig) {
    return { ok: false, status: 401, reason: "Malformed signature header" };
  }

  // Stale timestamp check: reject if older than 5 minutes -> 401
  const ts = Number(timestamp);
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (!Number.isFinite(ts) || now - ts > TIMESTAMP_TOLERANCE_SECONDS) {
    return { ok: false, status: 401, reason: "Stale or invalid timestamp" };
  }

  // Signed payload is exactly: timestamp + "." + raw_body
  const signedPayload = `${timestamp}.${req.rawBody.toString("utf8")}`;

  // Accept current OR previous secret. Compare against every candidate
  // (no early exit) so timing does not reveal which secret matched.
  const verified = WEBHOOK_SECRETS.some((secret) =>
    constantTimeEqual(receivedSig, computeHmac(secret, signedPayload))
  );
  if (!verified) {
    return { ok: false, status: 401, reason: "Invalid signature" };
  }

  // Replay check: repeated event ID within 10 minutes -> 409
  // (Runs only after authentication, so attackers cannot poison the store.)
  if (isDuplicateEvent(eventId)) {
    return { ok: false, status: 409, reason: "Duplicate event ID" };
  }

  return { ok: true, eventId };
}

// ---------- Express wiring ----------

export function captureRawBody(
  req: { rawBody?: Buffer },
  _res: unknown,
  buf: Buffer
): void {
  req.rawBody = buf; // must run before JSON parsing
}

export function webhookMiddleware(
  req: any,
  res: any,
  next: () => void
): void {
  const result = verifyWebhook({
    rawBody: req.rawBody,
    headers: req.headers,
  });

  if (!result.ok) {
    res.status(result.status).json({ error: result.reason });
    return;
  }
  next();
}

// app.use(express.json({ verify: captureRawBody }));
// app.post("/webhooks/provider", webhookMiddleware, handler);
```

Behavior summary:

| Condition | Status |
|---|---|
| Missing/malformed header, bad signature, stale timestamp (> 5 min old) | **401** |
| Valid signature but event ID seen within the last 10 minutes | **409** |
| Valid signature, fresh timestamp, unseen event ID | pass to handler |

Security properties preserved from the earlier iterations:

1. HMAC-SHA256 over `timestamp + "." + raw_body`, computed on the untouched raw bytes captured before body parsing.
2. Timestamps older than five minutes are rejected with 401 (replay-window protection).
3. Constant-time comparison via `crypto.timingSafeEqual`, with a length guard; the secret loop compares against all candidates with no early exit so timing doesn't reveal which rotated secret matched.
4. Secret rotation: current and previous secrets both accepted; remove the previous one after the rotation window.
5. Event-ID dedup (10-minute TTL) runs only after signature verification, so unauthenticated requests can't poison the dedup store. Swap the in-memory `Map` for Redis `SET ... NX PX 600000` when running multiple instances.

```javascript
function normalize(value) {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    return value.trim();
  }
  if (Array.isArray(value)) {
    return value.map(normalize);
  }
  if (value !== null && typeof value === "object") {
    const result = {};
    for (const key of Object.keys(value)) {
      result[key] = normalize(value[key]);
    }
    return result;
  }
  return value;
}
```

Behavior:
- **Strings** are trimmed.
- **Arrays** are recursively normalized element-wise, returning a new array.
- **Plain objects** (and other non-null objects) are recursively normalized per key, returning a new object.
- **Everything else** (numbers, booleans, `null`, `undefined`, functions) is returned unchanged.

Note: this treats any non-array object as a "plain object" branch. If you need to restrict to plain objects only (excluding `Date`, `Map`, class instances, etc.), let me know and I can add a stricter check like `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) === Object.prototype || Object.getPrototypeOf(obj) === null`.

```javascript
function normalize(value) {
  if (typeof value === "string") {
    const trimmed = value.trim();
