Node/Express version:

```javascript
const crypto = require("crypto");

const MAX_AGE_SECONDS = 300;

function verifyWebhook(req, res, next) {
  const sigHeader = req.get("X-Webhook-Signature") || "";
  const timestamp = req.get("X-Webhook-Timestamp") || "";

  const match = sigHeader.match(/^sha256=([0-9a-f]+)$/);
  if (!match) return res.status(400).send("Malformed signature");

  const ts = Number.parseInt(timestamp, 10);
  if (!Number.isInteger(ts)) return res.status(400).send("Malformed timestamp");

  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (Math.abs(now - ts) > MAX_AGE_SECONDS) {
    return res.status(401).send("Timestamp outside allowed window");
  }

  // Signed message: timestamp string + "." + raw body
  const expected = crypto.createHmac("sha256", process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET)
    .update(timestamp + "." + req.body) // req.body is the raw Buffer
    .digest("hex");

  const a = Buffer.from(expected, "hex");
  const b = Buffer.from(match[1], "hex");

  if (a.length !== b.length || !crypto.timingSafeEqual(a, b)) {
    return res.status(401).send("Invalid signature");
  }
  next();
}
```

Security notes on the changes:

- **Sign the header string, not the parsed timestamp.** If the sender signed `"07"` and you reconstruct from the parsed integer `7`, the HMAC won't match — and worse, lenient parsing could let an attacker mutate the timestamp. Use the raw header bytes verbatim.
- **`abs(now - ts)`** rejects both stale (replay) and far-future timestamps. A future-dated timestamp would otherwise extend the signature's usable lifetime beyond your window.
- **Validate the timestamp before the HMAC check** so malformed input fails fast — but note the freshness check alone must never be the only gate; the signature still binds the timestamp to the body, so an attacker can't swap in a fresh timestamp without invalidating the signature.
- **Constant-time comparison retained** (`hmac.compare_digest` / `timingSafeEqual` with the length pre-check).
- **Raw body still required** — the `.` separator means the body is concatenated directly, so any body transformation (JSON re-serialization, trailing-newline stripping, encoding changes) breaks verification.

One operational caveat: clock skew between your servers and the sender can cause false rejections at the window edge. If that's a concern, sync via NTP and consider a small skew tolerance rather than widening the window much — a larger window weakens replay protection.

Final consolidated version. All accumulated requirements: raw body, `timestamp + "." + raw_body` signing, 5-minute freshness window, constant-time comparison, dual-secret rotation, 10-minute replay cache, and now explicit status codes — **401** for bad signature/stale timestamp, **409** for replay.

```typescript
import crypto from "crypto";

// --- Configuration (from environment, never hardcoded) ---
const CURRENT_SECRET: string  = process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET!;
const PREVIOUS_SECRET: string | undefined = process.env.WEBHOOK_PREVIOUS_SECRET;

const MAX_AGE_SECONDS = 300;   // 5-minute freshness window
const REPLAY_TTL_SECONDS = 600; // 10-minute replay window (must be >= MAX_AGE_SECONDS)

// --- Replay cache: shared store (Redis) in production ---
// SET NX EX is atomic; an in-process Map only works for a single instance.
declare function redisSetIfAbsent(key: string, ttlSeconds: number): Promise<boolean>;

// --- Result type ---
type VerifyResult =
  | { status: 200 }
  | { status: 401; reason: string }
  | { status: 409; reason: string };

// --- Middleware ---
async function verifyWebhook(
  req: Request,
  res: Response,
  next: NextFunction
): Promise<void> {
  const signatureHeader = req.get("X-Webhook-Signature") ?? "";
  const timestamp       = req.get("X-Webhook-Timestamp") ?? "";
  const eventId         = req.get("X-Webhook-Event-ID") ?? "";

  // 1. Structural validation — fail fast, no secrets touched
  if (!signatureHeader || !timestamp || !eventId) {
    res.status(401).send("Missing required headers");
    return;
  }

  const sigMatch = signatureHeader.match(/^sha256=([0-9a-f]{64})$/);
  if (!sigMatch) {
    res.status(401).send("Malformed signature header");
    return;
  }
  const providedHex = sigMatch[1];

  if (!/^\d{1,15}$/.test(timestamp)) {
    res.status(401).send("Malformed timestamp");
    return;
  }
  if (!/^[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,64}$/.test(eventId)) {
    res.status(401).send("Malformed event ID");
    return;
  }

  // 2. Freshness check — stale or far-future => 401
  const ts  = Number.parseInt(timestamp, 10);
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (Math.abs(now - ts) > MAX_AGE_SECONDS) {
    res.status(401).send("Timestamp outside allowed window");
    return;
  }

  // 3. HMAC over BOTH secrets, computed unconditionally.
  //    Signed message is the raw header string + "." + raw body buffer.
  //    req.body must be the raw Buffer (express.raw mounted before this).
  const signedPayload = Buffer.concat([
    Buffer.from(timestamp, "utf8"),
    Buffer.from(".", "utf8"),
    req.body as Buffer,
  ]);

  const secrets = PREVIOUS_SECRET
    ? [CURRENT_SECRET, PREVIOUS_SECRET]
    : [CURRENT_SECRET];

  const expectedDigests: Buffer[] = secrets.map((secret) =>
    crypto.createHmac("sha256", secret).update(signedPayload).digest()
  );

  const provided = Buffer.from(providedHex, "hex");

  // 4. Constant-time comparison against every candidate.
  //    No short-circuiting on the secret dimension: check all digests
  //    so response timing doesn't reveal which secret matched.
  let signatureValid = false;
  for (const expected of expectedDigests) {
    if (
      expected.length === provided.length &&
      crypto.timingSafeEqual(expected, provided)
    ) {
      signatureValid = true;
      // do NOT break — evaluate all candidates for timing uniformity
    }
  }

  if (!signatureValid) {
    res.status(401).send("Invalid signature");
    return;
  }

  // 5. Replay check — AFTER authentication, atomic check-and-set.
  //    Never record unauthenticated event IDs (cache-poisoning DoS).
  const isNew = await redisSetIfAbsent(
    `webhook:seen:${eventId}`,
    REPLAY_TTL_SECONDS
  );

  if (!isNew) {
    res.status(409).send("Duplicate event");
    return;
  }

  next(); // verified, fresh, and first delivery — hand off to handler
}
```

Route wiring — raw body capture must precede JSON parsing:

```typescript
app.post(
  "/webhooks",
  express.raw({ type: "*/*", limit: "1mb" }),
  verifyWebhook,
  (req, res) => {
    const payload = JSON.parse((req.body as Buffer).toString("utf8"));
    // handle payload...
  }
);
```

Final security checklist, consolidated:

| Requirement | Enforcement |
|---|---|
| Raw body | `express.raw` before parser; HMAC over the untouched `Buffer` |
| `timestamp + "." + raw_body` | Concatenation uses the **raw header string**, not the parsed integer |
| 5-minute freshness | `abs(now - ts) > 300` → **401** (covers future-dated too) |
| Constant-time compare | `timingSafeEqual` with length pre-check; all candidate digests computed and compared, no short-circuit |
| Secret rotation | Current + previous both accepted; previous unset after overlap window (> freshness + sender retry) |
| 10-minute replay rejection | Atomic `SET NX EX` on event ID, checked **after** signature verification → **409** |
| Status codes | 401: missing/malformed headers, stale timestamp, bad signature. 409: verified but duplicate event |
| Cache hygiene | Event ID format-validated (bounded charset/length) to prevent key injection and unbounded growth; TTL ≥ freshness window so no gap between defenses |

One last operational note: monitor 409 rates per sender — a spike usually means their retry logic is misconfigured rather than an attack, and distinguishing the two saves you a false alarm.

